  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Ryan McMahon kept them alive with two outs and the game slipping away.",
    "what_this_means": "McMahon's three-run night was the difference in a one-run game that could have gone either way in the fourth inning. Toronto tied it right back, then the Yankees took the lead in the fifth and held it through nine. The bullpen did not give it away. Tim Hill, Jake Bird, Brent Headrick, and Camilo Doval combined for 3.1 scoreless innings when the margin was paper-thin. This is a team that converts one chance into a win rather than waiting for a blowout. They have now won back-to-back games. That matters less than the fact that they did it without dominating anything. They just refused to lose. Three straight one-run wins is not a formula. It is a warning light.",
    "what_to_watch": "Trey Yesavage brings a 1.40 ERA into tonight's matchup, and Cam Schlittler counters with a 0.77 mark over his last four starts. This is a starter's duel waiting to happen, and the team that scores first might not have to score twice.",
